{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"###Midline in sinusoids of the form $f(x)=a\\sin(bx+c)+d$\n\nGraphically, the midline of a sinusoidal function is the horizontal line that passes *exactly in the middle of its extreme values*.\n\nThe midline equation of a sinusoid of the form $f(x)={a}\\sin(bx + c) + \\greenD{d}$ is equal to $y=\\greenD{d}$. \n\n [[☃ explanation 2]]\n\n","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{"explanation 2":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"explanation":"Recall that the graph of $y=\\sin x$ has the following shape.\n\n![A graph of a trigonometric wave on an x y coordinate plane. The x and y axes scale by one. The midline is a dashed line at y equals zero. The graph passes through the midline at (zero, zero). The highest points on the graph go up to one on the y axis and the lowest points on the graph go to negative one on the y axis.](web+graphie:${☣ LOCALPATH}/images/a6e1c59c07682e53bd65207f142fa51af2ff729d)\n\nHere we see that the midline equation is $y=0$. \n\nThe graph of $y=\\sin( x)+d$ is the graph of $y=\\sin x$ shifted vertically $d$ units (up if $d\u003e0$ and down if $d\u003c0$).  Therefore the midline will also be shifted vertically $d$ units, and so the equation for the midline is $y=d$.  \n\nIn general, $y=a\\sin(bx+c)+d$ is a transformation of the graph of $y=\\sin x$.  However, the only transformation that will affect the halfway point between the two extremes (and thus the midline) is a vertical shift, which is determined by the variable $d$.  \n\nSo, the midline equation of $y=a\\sin(bx+c)+d$ is the same as the midline equation of $y=\\sin (x)+d$, which is $y=d$.","hidePrompt":"Got it, thanks!","showPrompt":"How can we justify this given our graphical understanding of midline?","static":false,"widgets":{"interaction 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"elements":[{"key":"line-8e8555","options":{"arrows":"","colo